ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176121 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176121 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176121 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176121 #edb/ref 1045 "The Extreme Value Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176121 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176121 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176121 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186176121 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "cac9bb11-d48e-5140-9653-cc117881f57c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176605 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176605 #edb/ref 1061 12791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176605 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9ff3fa5a-39c6-574d-bfa0-7e467bf44083" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176605 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176606 #edb/ref 1061 12792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176606 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8c5c661d-87d1-5fdd-b4cf-9b1217993d69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176606 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176607 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176607 #edb/ref 1061 12794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176607 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3001b2d1-c302-5b15-b3a6-3ff0fa36821e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176607 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176608 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176608 #edb/ref 1061 12795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176608 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7889a404-286a-5541-b477-ed0966b909de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176608 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176805 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176805 #edb/ref 1061 35242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176805 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa2543e5-fe3a-57fe-a964-118b017f51bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176805 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176968 #edb/ref 1064 "Under what conditions can we be sure that the global extrema of a function exist?\n\nThe **extreme value theorem** answers this question and states the following:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a continuous function defined on the closed interval $[a,b],$ then $f(x)$ always has at least one global maximum and one global minimum on $[a,b]$*.\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-3)^2, \\qquad x\\in [2,5].\n$$\n\nNotice that this function satisfies the conditions for the extreme value theorem:\n\n- the function $f(x)$ is *continuous* on the interval $x\\in [2,5]$, and\n\n- the interval $[2,5]$ is *closed*.\n\nSince *both* conditions are satisfied, the extreme value theorem guarantees that $f(x)$ must have at least one global maximum and one global minimum.\n\nTo see that this is indeed true, let's sketch the graph of $f(x).$ \n\n![](images/d2/d2ceffc40ead80b03d73004b663b91a942444bd14ce9e1e68fc98ffebbae3423.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the function $f(x)$ has \n\n- a global maximum at $x=5,$ and\n\n- a global minimum at $x=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176968 #edb/ref 1065 2666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176968 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "300a3bb8-a79f-5387-8c5e-1e403c2df531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176968 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177036 #edb/ref 1064 "We can only apply the extreme value theorem to *continuous* functions defined on *closed* intervals. Both conditions are crucial.\n\nTo see why this is the case, consider the function $g(x),$ given by\n\n$$\ng(x)=(x-3)^2, \\qquad x\\in [2,5).\n$$\n\nNotice that $g(x)$ is defined on a semi-open interval (the right endpoint is open). As a result, we cannot guarantee that $g(x)$ has a global maximum and a global minimum.\n\n![](images/a7/a7648d89fc02372f8d48a649425fa5dba4c8a86e12e9fa7bbddc69ffe52575c3.png)\n\nSimilarly, consider the function $h(x),$ given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}, & \\,2\\le x<3, \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+1, & \\,3\\le x\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $h(x)$ is defined on the closed interval $[2,5].$ However, since $h(x)$ is not continuous on $[2,5],$ we cannot guarantee that $h(x)$ has both a global maximum and minimum on $[2,5].$\n\n![](images/b1/b1bdc0a3c384e5918bdac46ce5db31c0836069eb8a3383febf2aac86654929ea.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177036 #edb/ref 1065 5745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177036 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "08bf8574-c1f7-5521-983b-282f7b0c3cc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177036 #edb/ref 1067 "Conditions for Applying the Extreme Value Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329840 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468516 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329840 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468517 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329840 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468518 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329840 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329840 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329840 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $[-1,1]$\n- **II.** $[0.5,1]$\n- **III.** $[-1,0)$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329840 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a410e5f1-db4f-5d99-8a2f-cd16595bdd74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329840 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-1,1].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now consider  $[0.5,1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[0.5,1].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[0.5,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $[-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341984 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468726 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186341984 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468727 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186341984 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468728 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186341984 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73481" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341984 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341984 #edb/ref 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{x}}{{x}^{2}+1}?$   \n\nI. $[-1,5]$\n\nII. $[0,2]$\n\nIII. $[2,5]$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186341984 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "122f233b-5382-5fef-8fbd-2b8682266e28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341984 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because $\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is not defined anywhere in the subinterval $[-1,0).$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The intervals $[0,2]$ and $[2,5]$ are closed, and the function is continuous on each of these intervals Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on each $[0,2]$  and $[2,5].$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341986 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468732 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186341986 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468733 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186341986 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468734 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186341986 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341986 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341986 #edb/ref 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}?$    \n\nI. $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nII. $[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n\nIII. $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186341986 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5aba1141-c0a4-54c1-bd64-30eb78786676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341986 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$  is closed, but the function is not defined on a part of the interval because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$    is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ is  closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468747 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186341993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468748 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186341993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468749 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186341993 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73503" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341993 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341993 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,2, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $[-1,1]$\n- **II.** $(0,3]$\n- **III.** $[-2,-1]$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186341993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a761269-f6ea-50eb-8e45-58983c3d9692" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341993 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- First, let's consider $[-1,1].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}=\\frac{2-0}{{2}^{0}+1}=1\\ne f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,-1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[-2,-1],$  and the extreme value theorem is also applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186429079 #edb/ref 1030 0.6666666666666666 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429079 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 2 rises / 3 eligible passe