 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508548 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "690abb71-4d77-510e-a85b-3cd428ff1d32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508576 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508576 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508576 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7f70533b-31ac-541b-be61-eba3464f5b2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508592 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508592 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508592 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8795d4c5-ffa2-5bdb-8d59-fab4fdcfe7bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508639 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508639 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508639 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a6b2db0b-70aa-51ed-a1c5-b48983c58ff7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508814 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508814 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508814 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508814 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0afcfbf6-047c-53c2-8a23-be6af1196c25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508954 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508954 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131070" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508954 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508954 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{2}-1}, & x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-5,1)$\n\nII. $[0,5]$\n\nIII. $[5,10]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508954 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "91c6ce97-3ba3-5af9-8b62-e9a8d179a50b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508954 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $[-5,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[0,5].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{x(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+1)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{1}{x+1})=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,5],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[5,10].$ In this interval $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[5,10].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509003 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186509024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509003 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0ea7f73f-9a2c-5b1b-92bd-1284bb0cf34e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509003 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186341986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509010 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186509047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509010 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "47653334-ddc8-59af-8924-8f0b31ddb36e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509010 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186329840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509024 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186509033 